ground_truth.body:
The salt length upper bound validation is a good security improvement to prevent DoS attacks. However, consider adding a validation to ensure the hashedPassword array has sufficient bytes to contain the declared saltLength before allocating the salt array. This would prevent unnecessary memory allocation and potential exception handling overhead for malformed payloads. Suggestion: Add a check `if (hashedPassword.Length < 13 + saltLength) { return false; }` before the salt allocation.

```suggestion
            // Read the salt: must be >= 128 bits
            if (saltLength < 128 / 8 || saltLength > MaxSaltSize)
            {
                return false;
            }
            // Verify the payload has enough bytes for the declared salt length
            if (hashedPassword.Length < 13 + saltLength)
            {
                return false;
            }
            byte[] salt = new byte[saltLength];
            Buffer.BlockCopy(hashedPassword, 13, salt, 0, salt.Length);
```

